# Google OAuth 2.0 Desktop client credentials.
# Obtain from Google Cloud Console → APIs & Services → Credentials → OAuth 2.0
# Client IDs (must be type "Desktop application"). The Gmail API must also be
# enabled for the same Cloud project.
#
# Both values are required by Google's Desktop OAuth flow. Google's docs state
# the client_secret for Desktop clients is not truly secret — it's embeddable
# in distributed binaries by design. We use PKCE on top to add per-flow
# protection (an intercepted auth code can't be exchanged without the
# matching code_verifier even if the secret is leaked).
#
# Copy this file to `.env` and fill in real values.
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=your-client-id.apps.googleusercontent.com
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=your-client-secret

# Android builds use a DIFFERENT OAuth client, and — despite the variable
# name — it must be of type "Web application", NOT "Android".
#
# The name says Android because that is which BUILD reads it; the type says Web
# because of what Google allows. Android receives its auth code over an https
# App Links URL (a plain loopback listener dies with the process when the OS
# evicts the app during consent), and only a Web-application client may declare
# an https redirect. A Desktop client is restricted to http://localhost, and
# the Android client type only offers a custom URI scheme, which Google has
# withdrawn.
#
# On the Web client, authorize exactly this redirect URI:
#     https://jodd.bbmedia.co.th/oauth2redirect
# It must match auth::ANDROID_REDIRECT_URI and the intent-filter generated from
# tauri.conf.json's deep-link config, character for character.
# See docs/android/APP-LINKS-SETUP.md for the assetlinks.json half.
#
# Leave both unset for desktop-only work — the build stays green without them.
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID_ANDROID=<your-web-oauth-client-id>.apps.googleusercontent.com
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET_ANDROID=<your-web-oauth-client-secret>

# Microsoft identity platform client id, for the Microsoft/Outlook backend.
# Register at Azure Portal → Microsoft Entra ID → App registrations.
#
# There is deliberately NO secret counterpart. This is a PUBLIC client: register
# it with "Allow public client flows" enabled, and the token exchange sends only
# client_id + the PKCE verifier. Sending a secret at all is a registration
# error here — unlike Google, whose Desktop/Web clients both carry one.
#
# The registration must be:
#   - Supported account types: multitenant + personal Microsoft accounts
#     (Apple syncs Notes to Outlook.com over Exchange, so consumer accounts are
#     the primary case)
#   - Platform "Mobile and desktop applications", redirect URI exactly:
#         http://localhost:8080
#     matching auth_ms::redirect_uri(). Port 8080 is shared with the Gmail flow;
#     only one sign-in runs at a time.
#   - Delegated permissions: Mail.ReadWrite, offline_access, User.Read.
#     offline_access is what makes a refresh token come back — without it the
#     user re-authenticates every hour and background sync is impossible.
#
# Users will see an "unverified" badge on the consent screen until publisher
# verification is done. Not blocking — consent was granted on both a personal
# @live.com account and a Microsoft 365 work account on 2026-08-14.
#
# Leave unset if you are not working on the Microsoft backend. Sign-in refuses
# with a message naming this variable rather than opening a browser that never
# redirects back (Microsoft answers AADSTS900144 to an empty client_id).
MS_CLIENT_ID=<your-microsoft-application-client-id>
